<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      The microphone, under the hardened runtime.

      Hardened runtime is what notarization requires and what turns every
      capability into something the binary has to ask for by name. Ara asks for
      exactly one: audio input. Everything else it does needs no entitlement —
      accessibility is a user grant rather than a capability, the models are
      files it reads, and the pasteboard belongs to whoever is frontmost.

      Deliberately not sandboxed. A sandboxed app cannot create the
      session-wide event tap the hotkey is built on, and cannot read the model
      cache that lives outside its container. That is a real limitation of this
      design, not an oversight: ara is distributed outside the App Store partly
      because of it.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
